      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-13T18:26:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So, this is essentially a lazy migration because we are allowing last_auth_at to be nullable in the migration but it should never be null once someone authenticates. That will always be the case, right? Do we ever have the need to change last_auth_at to not be nullable in a release or two?",
      "revId": "125df7d58bc8664e979fac2be8f71b9497945907",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_824b83b0",
        "filename": "keystone/identity/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 245,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-13T18:47:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Correct, however I think when you re-enable a user, you would set last_auth_at back to null.  Otherwise, they would be automatically disabled again.",
      "parentUuid": "7aa08908_1fd9b0f3",
      "revId": "125df7d58bc8664e979fac2be8f71b9497945907",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_2d041b13",
        "filename": "keystone/identity/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 259,
      "author": {
        "id": 5707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-14T12:57:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "the fact that we allow None here...does that mean that if, after migration, a user doesn\u0027t log in for 1 year....they will still be able to log in the first time, and that will start the clock ticking for 90 days? Is there a reason we don\u0027t set last_auth_at to now() in the migration?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 259,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 259,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "125df7d58bc8664e979fac2be8f71b9497945907",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_acc8bba9",
        "filename": "keystone/identity/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 259,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-15T18:46:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a good point. I didn\u0027t want to give last_auth_at a default value, because I didn\u0027t want to assume that the user actually had authenticated previously. Perhaps what I could do is if last_auth_at is None, use the date when the user was created. I\u0027ll give this some thought. Let me know if you have any ideas.",
      "parentUuid": "7aa08908_2d041b13",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 259,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 259,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "125df7d58bc8664e979fac2be8f71b9497945907",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
